add('A distance-regular intersection array fixes a conditional exact spectrum','intersection array','2026-10-05','T','Algebraic graph spectra',
'What spectrum follows from the recovered intersection array, and which additional graph data are needed to identify the transport operator?',
'The monograph supplies the array {6,5,1;1,1,6} and a relation B²=X+6I. These are useful exact constraints, but their operators must not be silently identified. The following result derives everything forced by the array under the explicit distance-regularity hypothesis.',
['Let A be the adjacency matrix of a connected distance-regular graph with intersection array {6,5,1;1,1,6}.','For a fixed vertex, b_i and c_i count neighbors moving outward and inward between its distance shells; a_i=6−b_i−c_i counts neighbors staying in the same shell.'],
'Conditional forty-two-vertex spectrum',
'Such a graph has 42 vertices and spectrum 6¹,2²¹,(−1)⁶,(−3)¹⁴. Its shell quotient is [[0,6,0,0],[1,0,5,0],[0,1,4,1],[0,0,6,0]], whose characteristic polynomial is (t−6)(t−2)(t+1)(t+3).',
'Counting edges between consecutive shells gives k_(i+1)c_(i+1)=k_i b_i. Starting k₀=1 produces shell sizes 1,6,30,5, hence 42 vertices. The stated quotient follows from the intersection numbers. The distance matrices A₀=I,A₁=A,A₂,A₃ satisfy a three-term multiplication recurrence; each A_i is a degree-i polynomial in A, and the final recurrence annihilates A with the quotient characteristic polynomial. The four nonempty disjoint distance matrices are linearly independent, so A has exactly four eigenvalues, precisely the quotient roots. Connected regularity makes eigenvalue six simple. If the other multiplicities are m₂,m_−1,m_−3, their sum is41, while tr A=0 and tr A²=42·6 give 6+2m₂−m_−1−3m_−3=0 and36+4m₂+m_−1+9m_−3=252. Solving yields21,6,14.',
'If the source relation uses B=A, derive the spectrum of X.',
'From X=A²−6I, the spectral mapping theorem gives X-eigenvalues 30,−2,−5,3 with multiplicities1,21,6,14. The trace is30−42−30+42=0, consistent with a zero diagonal. This conditional implication requires the actual identification B=A; the array by itself does not prove it.',
'The spectrum is obtained from exact combinatorial data and trace equations.',
'An admissible intersection array is not by itself an existence construction. No M22 embedding, mixing-angle exclusion or scanned-frame completeness is replayed without the actual graph and transport matrices.',
'Recover explicit adjacency and incidence matrices, verify every distance-shell count and B²=X+6I by integer multiplication, then compute invariant subspaces in the exact number field used for any residual-frame comparison.',
[('Brouwer, Cohen and Neumaier: distance-regular graph data','https://aeb.win.tue.nl/drg/index.html')])
add('Binary graph censuses can agree while integer-height series separate','Seven-vertex graphs','2026-10-17','A','Graph energy invariants',
'Can the retrieved seven-vertex collision be independently checked, including its first multilevel difference?',
'The exact edge lists make this a complete finite certificate rather than a qualitative graph-isomorphism claim. Binary heights see only cut boundaries. Integer heights add correlations invisible to that binary census and separate the supplied pair already at total height three.',
['On vertices0,…,6, take E(G)={03,04,06,12,13,23,24,35} and E(H)={01,04,06,15,16,26,36,45}.','Define E_G(h)=Σ_(ij∈E(G))(h_i−h_j)² and F_G(x,q)=Σ_(h∈Z_≥0^7)x^(Σh_i)q^(E_G(h)). A support-marked version also includes z^(#{i:h_i>0}).'],
'Exact binary collision and first height separation',
'The binary polynomials Σ_(h∈{0,1}⁷)x^(Σh_i)q^(E_G(h)) agree for G and H. Their integer-height series agree in degrees0,1,2, but [x³](F_G−F_H)=q³(1−q)(1−q³)²(1−q⁴)². The same difference is the coefficient x³z² in the support-marked version.',
'For a binary vector, each edge contributes one exactly when its endpoints lie on opposite sides of the selected subset. Thus the binary polynomial is obtained by enumerating the128 subsets and recording subset size and cut size. The verifier compares these exact integer histograms, not floating values at one q. At total height R, only finitely many weak compositions h₀+⋯+h₆=R occur, so direct enumeration gives the exact coefficient polynomial. For R=0,1,2 the histograms agree. For R=3 their subtraction has coefficient list q³−q⁴−2q⁶+2q⁸+q⁹+3q¹⁰−3q¹¹−q¹²−2q¹³+2q¹⁵+q¹⁷−q¹⁸, which multiplies out to the displayed factorization. Terms of support three are binary and cancel by the first identity. Terms of support one depend only on the degree census, which also agrees. Consequently the entire remaining difference has support two, proving the marked coefficient claim.',
'Evaluate the exact separation at q=1/2.',
'The factorization gives (1/8)(1/2)(7/8)²(15/16)²=11025/262144>0. Therefore the full height series differ, and graph isomorphism is impossible because relabeling preserves every energy coefficient.',
'A finite, exactly replayed collision disproves completeness of the binary census while giving a stronger distinguishing invariant.',
'No Euler-product singularity follows without a precisely defined prime product and analytic remainder bounds. The support-marked notation is explicitly defined here instead of assuming the unavailable original meaning of every series letter.',
'Classify the least total height separating all graphs in a fixed order, and compare this filtration with pinned or boundary observations; attach exact coefficient histograms to every claimed reconstruction.',
[('Andrén and Markström: the bivariate Ising polynomial','https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X09000821')])
add('The centered graph Green matrix gives a sharp constrained energy cost','K_G=(L_G','2026-10-19','A','Graph quadratic forms',
'Why do the recovered constants5,15/2 and3 arise from a centered resolvent, and which energy model do they belong to?',
'The source gives a graph resolvent and reciprocal diagonal constants. These admit a sharp finite-dimensional variational proof. It is important to distinguish quadratic regularization, used here, from the separately recovered series with the linear term λΣh_i.',
['For a graph on n≥2 vertices, let L be its Laplacian and λ>0. Put Q=L+λI, Π=I−11ᵀ/n and K=Q⁻¹−11ᵀ/(λn).','For real v, vᵀQv=Σ_(ij∈E)(v_i−v_j)²+λΣ_i v_i². Restrict to the mean-zero space1⊥.'],
'Sharp one-coordinate energy minimization',
'K=ΠQ⁻¹Π is positive definite on1⊥. For each vertex i and real a, min{vᵀQv:1ᵀv=0,v_i=a}=a²/K_ii, attained at v=aKe_i/K_ii. Thus the least energy for a coordinate of magnitude a is a²/max_iK_ii.',
'Since L1=0, Q⁻¹1=λ⁻¹1, which proves the formula with Π. On1⊥, Q is positive definite and K is its inverse. For v in that space, e_iᵀv=(Ke_i)ᵀQv because QKe_i=Πe_i. Cauchy–Schwarz in the Q-inner product gives |v_i|²≤[(Ke_i)ᵀQ(Ke_i)]vᵀQv=K_ii vᵀQv. Equality occurs for a scalar multiple of Ke_i; its ith component fixes the scalar to a/K_ii. Since Πe_i is nonzero for n≥2, K_ii>0. Taking the best vertex proves the final statement. Uniqueness can also be checked without an inequality: every other admissible vector is v+u with u_i=0 and mean zero. The cross term vᵀQu=(a/K_ii)(Πe_i)ᵀu vanishes, leaving the strictly positive increment uᵀQu unless u=0. This is an exact Euler–Lagrange certificate for the constrained minimizer.',
'Compute λ=2 for three three-vertex graphs.',
'For the path P₃, diag K=(1/5,2/15,1/5), so1/max K_ii=5. For the triangle, K=(1/5)Π, giving diag2/15 and reciprocal15/2. For an edge plus an isolated vertex, diag K=(5/24,5/24,1/3), giving reciprocal3. The verifier reconstructs L and inverts Q exactly over Q.',
'The three recovered constants are reproduced as rigorous quadratic-energy minima.',
'This does not prove a theta-tail asymptotic or identify the linear-weight height series F_(G,λ) with a quadratic Gaussian series. Integer-lattice minimization and asymptotic prefactors require further arguments beyond the real variational minimum.',
'For integer mean-constrained heights, compare the sharp real minimizer with a nearest lattice point and bound the bounded rounding correction; only then derive any logarithmic tail rate with the exact series definition stated.',
[('Boyd and Vandenberghe: quadratic optimization','https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/')])
add('Finite observation cutoffs need a stated algebraic or linear closure theorem','Noetherianity','2026-10-18','A','Algebraic reconstruction',
'When does a finite observation prefix determine an infinite series, and can the cutoff be computed?',
'The retrieved work separates a Noetherian existence claim from a finite-dimensional boundary-state closure. These are different kinds of certificate. One may prove that some finite prefix suffices without obtaining any effective numerical bound; a supplied finite transition system permits an explicit rank-based stopping test.',
['Let a_n(θ) be polynomials over a field k in a fixed finite parameter tuple θ. Compare two parameter tuples θ and θ′.','For a separate linear model, let W₀ be a row space in k^m and M₁,…,M_r fixed matrices; define W_(j+1)=W_j+Σ_i W_jM_i.'],
'Two precise finite-determination principles',
'There exists N such that equality a_n(θ)=a_n(θ′) for0≤n≤N implies equality for every n, with θ,θ′ in any field extension of k. In the linear model, the chain W_j stabilizes after at most m−dim W₀ strict increases; once stable, it contains every row wM_word with w∈W₀.',
'In k[θ,θ′], let I_N be the ideal generated by a_n(θ)−a_n(θ′) for n≤N. Hilbert’s basis theorem makes this polynomial ring Noetherian, so the ascending ideal chain stabilizes at some I_N. Every later coefficient difference is then a polynomial combination of the first N+1 differences, and vanishes at any pair of parameter values making those differences zero. This proves existence but supplies no general effective N. For the linear claim, dimensions cannot strictly increase more than m−dim W₀ times. If one step has W_(j+1)=W_j, then W_j is invariant under all right multiplications M_i; induction over word length includes every future row. Thus exact row reduction and explicit spanning identities give a computable closure certificate.',
'Use the rational family F_(a,b)(x)=a/(1−bx).',
'Its coefficients are a_n=abⁿ. Equality of a₀ and a₁ determines all coefficients: if a≠0 it determines b, while if a=0 the entire series vanishes independently of b. Therefore this chosen family has cutoff N=1, although its parameters are not identifiable on a=0.',
'Finite series determination and parameter reconstruction are distinct conclusions.',
'The theorem applies to a fixed finite polynomial parameterization; it gives no uniform cutoff across unbounded templates or arbitrary sequences. The historical22-dimensional closure ranks cannot be replayed without their matrices.',
'For the actual graph-template family, supply an explicit generating ideal or boundary transition representation and derive an effective cutoff; separately prove when equality of the resulting invariant forces graph isomorphism in the stated graph class.',
[('Stacks Project: Hilbert basis theorem','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FM')])
